[2026-03-14T19:54:28.226Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GxpptPDjUQ8K2vtfh79Uki","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# devServer\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→Documents the standard procedure for starting and stopping the Netlify dev server, including\n     6→health-check polling to wait for readiness. Reduces `curl` failures caused by hitting endpoints\n     7→before the server is ready.\n     8→\n     9→## Starting the Dev Server\n    10→\n    11→**Always kill existing processes and free ports before starting.** This eliminates the #1\n    12→source of dev server failures:\n    13→\n    14→```bash\n    15→pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n    16→```\n    17→\n    18→Then start the dev server in the background from the app directory:\n    19→\n    20→```bash\n    21→npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions &\n    22→```\n    23→\n    24→**IMPORTANT**: Always pass `--functions ./netlify/functions` to avoid 404 errors on function\n    25→endpoints. The `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions directory to resolve\n    26→incorrectly without this flag.\n    27→\n    28→## Health-Check Polling\n    29→\n    30→After starting the dev server, wait for it to be ready before making requests. Use this\n    31→polling pattern with a timeout:\n    32→\n    33→```bash\n    34→for i in $(seq 1 30); do curl -s http://localhost:8888 > /dev/null && break; sleep 1; done\n    35→```\n    36→\n    37→This tries once per second for up to 30 seconds. If the server is not ready after 30 seconds,\n    38→investigate — check for port conflicts or startup errors in the terminal output.\n    39→\n    40→For use in TypeScript scripts, poll with `fetch`:\n    41→\n    42→```ts\n    43→async function waitForServer(url: string, timeoutMs = 30000): Promise<void> {\n    44→  const start = Date.now();\n    45→  while (Date.now() - start < timeoutMs) {\n    46→    try {\n    47→      await fetch(url);\n    48→      return;\n    49→    } catch {\n    50→      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000));\n    51→    }\n    52→  }\n    53→  throw new Error(`Server not ready after ${timeoutMs}ms`);\n    54→}\n    55→```\n    56→\n    57→## Stopping the Dev Server\n    58→\n    59→Always clean up dev server processes when done. Use error suppression to avoid failures when\n    60→no process is running:\n    61→\n    62→```bash\n    63→pkill -f \"netlify dev\" 2>/dev/null || true\n    64→pkill -f \"vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n    65→```\n    66→\n    67→The `|| true` ensures the command succeeds even if no matching process exists, avoiding\n    68→spurious failures in scripts.\n    69→\n    70→## Common Issues\n    71→\n    72→- **`curl` returns connection refused**: The server is not yet ready. Use the health-check\n    73→  polling pattern above instead of a single `curl`.\n    74→- **Port already in use**: A previous dev server is still running. Kill it first with the\n    75→  cleanup commands above.\n    76→- **Zombie processes**: `<defunct>` processes from previous runs are harmless and cannot be\n    77→  killed. They do not affect new server instances on different ports.\n    78→\n    79→## When to Use Manual Dev Server\n    80→\n    81→- **Testing**: Do NOT start the dev server manually. The test script (`npm run test`) manages\n    82→  the server automatically. See `skills/scripts/test.md`.\n    83→- **One-off curl checks**: Start manually, verify the endpoint, then stop immediately.\n    84→- **Debugging API endpoints**: Start manually to test function responses with `curl`.\n    85→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a786a10b-f37f-4561-9861-ba9a77a4272d","uuid":"6b3f4bd8-076f-4a77-ae7c-c521b2f9c86c","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/scripts/devServer.md","content":"# devServer\n\n## Purpose\n\nDocuments the standard procedure for starting and stopping the Netlify dev server, including\nhealth-check polling to wait for readiness. Reduces `curl` failures caused by hitting endpoints\nbefore the server is ready.\n\n## Starting the Dev Server\n\n**Always kill existing processes and free ports before starting.** This eliminates the #1\nsource of dev server failures:\n\n```bash\npkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n```\n\nThen start the dev server in the background from the app directory:\n\n```bash\nnpx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions &\n```\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Always pass `--functions ./netlify/functions` to avoid 404 errors on function\nendpoints. The `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions directory to resolve\nincorrectly without this flag.\n\n## Health-Check Polling\n\nAfter starting the dev server, wait for it to be ready before making requests. Use this\npolling pattern with a timeout:\n\n```bash\nfor i in $(seq 1 30); do curl -s http://localhost:8888 > /dev/null && break; sleep 1; done\n```\n\nThis tries once per second for up to 30 seconds. If the server is not ready after 30 seconds,\ninvestigate — check for port conflicts or startup errors in the terminal output.\n\nFor use in TypeScript scripts, poll with `fetch`:\n\n```ts\nasync function waitForServer(url: string, timeoutMs = 30000): Promise<void> {\n  const start = Date.now();\n  while (Date.now() - start < timeoutMs) {\n    try {\n      await fetch(url);\n      return;\n    } catch {\n      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000));\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error(`Server not ready after ${timeoutMs}ms`);\n}\n```\n\n## Stopping the Dev Server\n\nAlways clean up dev server processes when done. Use error suppression to avoid failures when\nno process is running:\n\n```bash\npkill -f \"netlify dev\" 2>/dev/null || true\npkill -f \"vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n```\n\nThe `|| true` ensures the command succeeds even if no matching process exists, avoiding\nspurious failures in scripts.\n\n## Common Issues\n\n- **`curl` returns connection refused**: The server is not yet ready. Use the health-check\n  polling pattern above instead of a single `curl`.\n- **Port already in use**: A previous dev server is still running. Kill it first with the\n  cleanup commands above.\n- **Zombie processes**: `<defunct>` processes from previous runs are harmless and cannot be\n  killed. They do not affect new server instances on different ports.\n\n## When to Use Manual Dev Server\n\n- **Testing**: Do NOT start the dev server manually. The test script (`npm run test`) manages\n  the server automatically. See `skills/scripts/test.md`.\n- **One-off curl checks**: Start manually, verify the endpoint, then stop immediately.\n- **Debugging API endpoints**: Start manually to test function responses with `curl`.\n","numLines":85,"startLine":1,"totalLines":85}}}
