[2026-03-14T19:54:28.247Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NFRFH6W9522hpWmzbC1etM","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# seed-db\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→Seeds the database with initial data. The seed script lives at `scripts/seed-db.ts` in each app.\n     6→\n     7→## Usage\n     8→\n     9→```bash\n    10→npx tsx scripts/seed-db.ts\n    11→```\n    12→\n    13→## Known Issues\n    14→\n    15→### Existing data causes failures\n    16→\n    17→The seed script may fail if data already exists in the database (e.g., duplicate key errors).\n    18→This happens when re-seeding after a previous run or when Neon branches inherit parent data.\n    19→\n    20→**Workaround**: Clear existing data before re-seeding:\n    21→\n    22→```sql\n    23→-- Run via npx tsx -e or a cleanup script\n    24→TRUNCATE table1, table2, table3 CASCADE;\n    25→```\n    26→\n    27→Then re-run the seed script.\n    28→\n    29→### Best practice\n    30→\n    31→Seed scripts should always call `truncateAllTables()` (or equivalent) before inserting data\n    32→to ensure idempotency. If the app's seed script does not do this, consider adding a\n    33→`--clean` or `--force` flag that truncates all tables before seeding.\n    34→\n    35→### Pre-test re-seeding\n    36→\n    37→Always re-seed the database before running test suites to ensure a clean, known state. The\n    38→test script (`npm run test`) handles this automatically via ephemeral Neon branches with\n    39→truncate-and-seed between each test. If running tests manually or debugging, re-seed\n    40→explicitly:\n    41→\n    42→```bash\n    43→npx tsx scripts/seed-db.ts\n    44→```\n    45→\n    46→This eliminates the #2 source of test failures (data contamination from accumulated state).\n    47→\n    48→### When to use `scripts/seed-db.ts` vs `/api/seed`\n    49→\n    50→- **`npx tsx scripts/seed-db.ts`**: Use for initial database setup, manual re-seeding before\n    51→  test runs, and when the dev server is not running. Runs directly against the database.\n    52→- **`POST /api/seed` endpoint**: Use inside test `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` hooks to reset\n    53→  state between tests while the dev server is running. This is the preferred approach for\n    54→  in-test reseeding because it uses the same database connection as the running server and\n    55→  avoids connection conflicts. Many test failures trace back to stale seed data — using the\n    56→  `/api/seed` endpoint in `beforeEach` hooks is the most effective mitigation.\n    57→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a786a10b-f37f-4561-9861-ba9a77a4272d","uuid":"ba4c19e7-24b4-4d0f-ba33-690b5201a8ef","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/scripts/seed-db.md","content":"# seed-db\n\n## Purpose\n\nSeeds the database with initial data. The seed script lives at `scripts/seed-db.ts` in each app.\n\n## Usage\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx scripts/seed-db.ts\n```\n\n## Known Issues\n\n### Existing data causes failures\n\nThe seed script may fail if data already exists in the database (e.g., duplicate key errors).\nThis happens when re-seeding after a previous run or when Neon branches inherit parent data.\n\n**Workaround**: Clear existing data before re-seeding:\n\n```sql\n-- Run via npx tsx -e or a cleanup script\nTRUNCATE table1, table2, table3 CASCADE;\n```\n\nThen re-run the seed script.\n\n### Best practice\n\nSeed scripts should always call `truncateAllTables()` (or equivalent) before inserting data\nto ensure idempotency. If the app's seed script does not do this, consider adding a\n`--clean` or `--force` flag that truncates all tables before seeding.\n\n### Pre-test re-seeding\n\nAlways re-seed the database before running test suites to ensure a clean, known state. The\ntest script (`npm run test`) handles this automatically via ephemeral Neon branches with\ntruncate-and-seed between each test. If running tests manually or debugging, re-seed\nexplicitly:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx scripts/seed-db.ts\n```\n\nThis eliminates the #2 source of test failures (data contamination from accumulated state).\n\n### When to use `scripts/seed-db.ts` vs `/api/seed`\n\n- **`npx tsx scripts/seed-db.ts`**: Use for initial database setup, manual re-seeding before\n  test runs, and when the dev server is not running. Runs directly against the database.\n- **`POST /api/seed` endpoint**: Use inside test `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` hooks to reset\n  state between tests while the dev server is running. This is the preferred approach for\n  in-test reseeding because it uses the same database connection as the running server and\n  avoids connection conflicts. Many test failures trace back to stale seed data — using the\n  `/api/seed` endpoint in `beforeEach` hooks is the most effective mitigation.\n","numLines":57,"startLine":1,"totalLines":57}}}
